INT. CURLING RINK - ICE LEVEL - NIGHT

A cold hush. Brooms squeak. Scoreboard: 7-7, last end.

COACH GRETA LINDQVIST, 50s, in a parka, paces behind the hog line. DOC HOLLIS, 60s, the team physio, hovers with a thermos. JUNIOR ABIODUN, 19, kneels in the hack, stone in hand. MATEO CRUZ, 30s, sweeper, and SIGRID, 20s, sweeper, hold their brooms like rifles.

GRETA
(to the team)
Breathe. Everybody breathe.

JUNIOR
(muttering)
Don't blow it. Don't blow it.

DOC
You've got it, kid.

MATEO
Line's perfect.

SIGRID
Line's slow.

GRETA
Sigrid, call it.

SIGRID
Hard! Hard! Hard!

Junior releases. The stone glides. Mateo and Sigrid sweep, brooms a blur.

MATEO
Off! Off! Off!

SIGRID
No, leave it!

GRETA
Leave it, leave it, leave it!

Doc turns away, can't watch. The stone creeps toward the button. Stops. A hair short of the rival stone.

Silence. Then the horn.

DOC
(whispering)
It's good.

JUNIOR
Is it good?

The rink erupts. Mateo hurls his broom. Sigrid sobs into Greta's parka.

GRETA
(muttering, to herself)
Thank God.
